{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["U2"],
      "pins": ["U2.50 (VREF+)"],
      "nets": ["VREF+", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the top sheet (PCB.kicad_sch) the four bottom-edge pins of U2 are labelled VSS, VREF-, VREF+ and VSSA, and all four stubs are joined by a single horizontal wire that terminates in the GND symbol. Thus the VREF+ reference pin is tied directly to GND, while VDDA (pin 47) is tied to VCC.",
      "expected": "Standard practice for the STM32L476 (and the spec's instruction to judge silent pins by good practice) requires VREF+ to be driven from the same rail as VDDA (VCC), with only VSS/VREF-/VSSA on GND. Tying VREF+ to GND contradicts this.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Top sheet, U2 bottom edge: VSS / VREF- / VREF+ / VSSA stubs all merge into one wire ending at the GND symbol (PCB.kicad_sch)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "The specification is silent on VREF+/VREF-; it directs that 'Where it is silent, judge the design with standard electrical-engineering practice.'"}
      ],
      "impact": "The ADC/touch-sensing positive reference is forced to 0 V, so any analog measurement referenced to VREF+ is invalid; it also creates an unintended low-impedance path on the analog reference pin.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The NFC, display, touch, SWD and power sections otherwise match the specification (correct pull-up values, BS0/BS1/BS2 strapping, D0/D1/D2 I2C wiring, VOUT-to-VCC, C18='??', TSC topology, decoupling values). The one deviation I can read off the drawing is that U2's VREF+ pin is tied to the GND rail together with VSS/VREF-/VSSA, whereas it should be connected to VDDA/VCC; confidence is moderate because the bottom pin group is small on the drawing."
}

